  "output": "This technical analysis evaluates the queue depth incident observed between 09:00 and 09:35, utilizing the provided synthetic evidence logs (E01–E10). The incident was characterized by an exponential surge in task latency and queue accumulation, which ultimately proved resistant to horizontal scaling but highly sensitive to retry policy adjustments.\n\nThe primary root cause of the degradation was identified as external rate limiting imposed by outbound Dependency B. Evidence E05 indicates that at 09:15, Dependency B began rejecting 31% of requests with HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests). While this rejection rate alone would typically cause a moderate increase in error rates, the severity of the resulting queue backlog suggests a secondary amplification factor: the system’s retry logic. As noted in E06, the system employed exponential backoff with up to three retries. When combined with the high volume of tasks hitting the rate limit, this created a 'retry storm,' where each original request generated multiple additional requests that further saturated Dependency B's capacity limits.\n\nThe distinction between the root cause (Dependency B rate limits) and the amplifiers (aggressive retry logic) is critical. Resource utilization data in E04 shows CPU at 43%, memory at 51%, and disk at 22%, confirming that the local compute environment was not the bottleneck. This is further supported by E07, where doubling the worker count from 8 to 16 resulted in only a 6% reduction in queue depth over ten minutes. If the issue were local processing or thread contention, adding workers would have provided near-linear relief. Instead, the additional workers simply increased the velocity of requests hitting the constrained upstream dependency, exacerbating the 429 response rate.\n\nConversely, the efficacy of disabling retries (E08), which led to a 38% drop in queue depth within six minutes, confirms that the majority of the queue occupancy consisted of tasks stuck in retry loops. Once the rate limits on Dependency B were adjusted (E09), the system rapidly stabilized, with queue depths falling below baseline levels. The absence of any internal changes (E10) rules out deployment-related regressions or credential failures, narrowing the focus strictly to the interaction between the client-side retry strategy and server-side throttling.\n\nIn conclusion, the incident was a classic case of a thundering herd problem triggered by external throttling. The remediation path involves both immediate operational adjustments—specifically implementing circuit breakers for 429 responses—and strategic architectural shifts toward more graceful degradation and adaptive rate-limiting awareness.",
