    "executive_summary": "Between 09:00 and 09:35, the system experienced a severe performance degradation characterized by a 15-fold increase in queue depth and an 8-fold increase in median task latency. Investigation revealed that the incident was not caused by local resource exhaustion or internal code deployments, but rather by an interaction between external rate limiting on Dependency B and an overly aggressive internal retry mechanism. Dependency B began returning HTTP 429 errors for approximately 31% of traffic, triggering a 420% surge in retry attempts. These retries consumed significant worker capacity without successfully completing tasks, leading to a rapid queue buildup. Notably, scaling the worker pool proved ineffective because the bottleneck was external, whereas temporarily suppressing retries provided immediate relief. The resolution involved coordinating with the Dependency B team to increase rate limits, after which the system returned to normal operational baselines. Future stability requires the implementation of intelligent circuit breaking and jittered backoff strategies to prevent similar feedback loops during upstream throttling events.",
